   408	                              §5. Proof of Theorem 1.1
   409	Lemma 5.1. If a group G contains a subgroup G1 isomorphic to a free group of
   410	rank greater than one, then G cannot contain any subgroup of finite index isomor-
   411	phic to Z.
   412	Proof. Let us denote two of free generators of G1 by a and b. If H is a subgroup
   413	of G of finite index, then some powers of the elements a and b must lie in H.
   414	Therefore, if H were isomorphic to Z then there would exist integers m and n such
   415	that am = bn . But this contradicts the fact that a and b are free generators of G1 .
   416	Lemma 5.2. Let x ∈ X      e ∗ , x 6= ∞ (see §4). Suppose that x lies on the image
   417	l∗ = π(l) of an irreducible component l of LF . If µx f ∗ = µl f ∗ , then a germ of the
   418	mapping f ∗ |l∗ at x determines a nonsingular branch of the curve f ∗ (l∗ ) at f ∗ (x);
   419	that is, there exists a neighbourhood U of x such that f ∗ ◦ π|U∩l is a nonsingular
   420	embedding.

