    11	                                                                                Abstract
    12	                                                    We are concerned with the behavior of the polynomial maps F =
    13	                                                (P, Q) of C2 with finite fibres and satisfying the condition that all of the
    14	                                                curves aP + bQ = 0, (a : b) ∈ P1 , are irreducible rational curves. The
    15	                                                obtained result shows that such polynomial maps F is invertible if (0, 0)
    16	                                                is a regular value of F or if the Jacobian condition holds.
    17	                                                    Keywords and Phrases: Plane Jacobian conjecture, Polynomial auto-
    18	                                                morphism, Pencil of irreducible rational curves.
    19	                                                    2000 Mathematical Subject Classification: Primary 14R15, 14R25; Sec-
    20	                                                ondary 14E20.
    21	                                          1. The mysterious Jacobian Conjecture (see [1] and [2] for its history and
    22	                                          surveys), posed first by Ott-Heinrich Keller [7] since 1939 and remains open
    23	                                          even for the case n = 2, asserts that every polynomial map F of Cn satisfying
    24	                                          the Jacobian condition det DF ≡ const. 6= 0 is invertible, and hence, is a
    25	                                          polynomial automorphism of Cn . The following results, which appeared in the
    26	                                          literature in some convenient statements, characterize the invertibility of non-
    27	                                          zero constant Jacobian polynomial maps F in terms of the topology of inverse
    28	                                          images F −1 (l) of the complex lines l ⊂ Cn ,
    29	                                          Theorem 1. Let F be a polynomial map of Cn with non-zero constant Jaco-
    30	                                          bian, det DF ≡ const. 6= 0. Then,
    31	                                             i) F is invertible if the inverse images F −1 (l) of complex lines l ⊂ Cn having
    32	                                                same a fixed direction are irreducible rational curves, and
    33	                                            ii) F is invertible if for generic point q ∈ Cn the inverse images F −1 (l) of
    34	                                                complex lines l ⊂ Cn passing through q are irreducible rational curves.
    35	                                             Here, we mean an irreducible rational curve to be an algebraic curve home-
    36	                                          omorphic to the 2-dimensional sphere with a finite number of punctures.
    37	                                             Theorem 1 (ii), due to Nollet and Xavier (Corollary 1.3, [13]), is deduced
    38	                                          from a deep result on the holomorphic injectivity (Theorem 1.1, in [13]). Theo-
    39	                                          rem 1 (i) appears earlier with algebraic and algebra-geometric proofs in Razar
